<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalanimoku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1768–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as William Pitt Kalanimoku, Kalaimoku, Karaimoku, Iron Cable of Hawaiʻi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalanimoku, who took the name William Pitt, was a Hawaiian high chief who served much like a prime minister under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Kamehameha II</a> and early in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a>. Born on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> around 1768, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kekaulike">Kekaulike</a> of Maui, he was captured by Kamehameha's forces at the Battle of Mokuohai but spared, and became his chief minister and treasurer. In 1819 he was baptised a Roman Catholic aboard the French ship Uranie, and in December that year he led the army that defeated Kekuaokalani's revolt at Kuamoʻo. He was regent with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> from 1823 and died at Kailua Kona in 1827; for his abilities he was called the Iron Cable of Hawaiʻi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the land again, and the chiefs who backed Kamehameha after 1782 took the lands of the defeated: his ally Keʻeaumoku Pāpaʻiahiahi, once a fugitive, became governor of Maui, and <b>Kalanimoku</b>, a young chief captured on the losing side at Mokuʻōhai, rose to be Kamehameha’s chief minister and treasurer.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.77,-156.27&amp;year=1806" aria-label="Maui on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="345 1395 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kalanimoku"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M795 1665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M795 1665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.77,-156.27&amp;year=1806">What was here in 1806? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
